# 1Password secret references — SAFE TO COMMIT.
#
# This file holds no secrets: every value is a pointer into a vault, resolved at
# run time by `op run --env-file=.env.op -- <command>`. The resolved values live
# in the launched process's memory for its lifetime and nowhere else — not in
# .env.local, not in your shell, not in a dotfile backup.
#
# It is also documentation: it states which secrets this project consumes and
# where they live, which a .env.example can only suggest.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Install the 1Password CLI and enable "Integrate with 1Password CLI" in
#      the desktop app (Settings -> Developer). That is what makes the biometric
#      prompt appear.
#   2. Find your vault — the name below is one of ours, not a default:
#        op vault list
#   3. If `op account list` shows more than one account, set OP_ACCOUNT in
#      .env.local. A reference names a vault, never an account, so with a work
#      and a personal account signed in, `op` resolves against whichever it
#      considers current — and a personal project's key belongs in the personal
#      account.
#   4. Create the items below, or edit the references to match yours. Check one —
#      pass --account explicitly, because your shell does not source .env.local
#      and `op` otherwise resolves against whichever account it considers
#      current, reporting a missing *vault* when the *account* is the problem:
#        op read --account <address> "op://Personal/anthropic-epub-translator/credential"
#
# Adding a secret here is only half the work: the recipe that needs it must be
# wrapped in `op run` in the Justfile. Recipes that make no provider call —
# `estimate`, `glossary-suggest`, `eval-ref` — are deliberately left unwrapped,
# so pricing a book or extracting proper nouns never asks for a credential.

# LLM chat provider (literary mode, style-profile, series-bible).
#
# ONE reference at a time: the pipeline has a single LLM_API_KEY, so the active
# line must match EPUB_TRANSLATOR_LLM_CHAT_PROVIDER in .env.local. Swapping
# provider means swapping which line is commented out here — a stale pairing
# sends an Anthropic key to DeepSeek and fails as an authentication error, which
# reads like a bad key rather than a mismatched one.
#
# With LLM_CHAT_PROVIDER=claude-cli, no key is needed at all: comment both out
# and the recipes stop asking for a credential entirely.
EPUB_TRANSLATOR_LLM_API_KEY=op://Personal/anthropic-epub-translator/credential
# EPUB_TRANSLATOR_LLM_API_KEY=op://Personal/deepseek-epub-translator/credential

# NMT draft provider. Required by `run`/`batch` when NMT_PROVIDER=google;
# ignored by the self-hosted engines.
EPUB_TRANSLATOR_NMT_API_KEY=op://Personal/gcp-cloud-translation/credential

# A hosted COMET-QE service would go here too — a local one needs no key:
# EPUB_TRANSLATOR_COMET_API_KEY=op://Personal/comet-qe/credential
